WEBVTT

1
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.200
Now that we've introduced
you to some of

2
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.970
the mechanics of trading Futures,

3
00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:27.045
including the roles of
commoditized contracts,

4
00:00:27.045 --> 00:00:29.830
let's turn your attention
to the use of margin.

5
00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:32.060
Most Futures contracts require

6
00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:34.475
investors to post margin
with their broker.

7
00:00:34.475 --> 00:00:37.610
While this is a good faith
deposit required of investors

8
00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:39.380
to help ensure they abide by

9
00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.570
the rules set by the exchange,

10
00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:44.180
brokerage companies can
ask their clients to

11
00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.390
set aside more than
this minimum amount.

12
00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:50.270
The exchange monitors
the price variation

13
00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:52.025
of its contracts over time,

14
00:00:52.025 --> 00:00:53.750
and we'll set the
margin according to

15
00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.539
the volatility of
each underlying.

16
00:00:56.539 --> 00:00:59.960
Investors should be aware
that margin requirements can,

17
00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.120
and will change
from time to time,

18
00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.670
especially in the event
of market volatility.

19
00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:06.740
The exchange is typically

20
00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:09.320
ambivalent to long
or short positions,

21
00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:12.935
and charges the same margin
requirement either way.

22
00:01:12.935 --> 00:01:14.990
Again, brokerage
firms can augment

23
00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:17.800
the rules on this front
should they choose.

24
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:20.235
To establish the
margin requirement,

25
00:01:20.235 --> 00:01:22.169
exchange is typically calculate

26
00:01:22.169 --> 00:01:24.770
in post-product price thresholds.

27
00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:26.360
That is the maximum permitted

28
00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.520
daily price move per product,

29
00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.665
and use the value of
that price change

30
00:01:30.665 --> 00:01:32.465
as the required margin.

31
00:01:32.465 --> 00:01:34.610
In other words, should
the price of a contract

32
00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:36.965
fall by its maximum
permitted value,

33
00:01:36.965 --> 00:01:39.485
causing the exchange
to suspend trading,

34
00:01:39.485 --> 00:01:41.930
investors holding long
positions would face

35
00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:46.135
losses exactly equal to
their margin requirement.

36
00:01:46.135 --> 00:01:47.610
You may also want to keep in mind

37
00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:49.340
that Futures provide extremely

38
00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:50.480
efficient and relatively

39
00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:52.475
low-cost investment
opportunities,

40
00:01:52.475 --> 00:01:54.410
providing tools for hedging and

41
00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:57.295
speculation without ever
owning the underlying.

42
00:01:57.295 --> 00:01:59.960
As the Futures markets
continue to undergo

43
00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:03.155
ongoing transformation
to electronic trading,

44
00:02:03.155 --> 00:02:06.605
trading costs have
been trending lower.

45
00:02:06.605 --> 00:02:08.570
Now overall, you should be more

46
00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:10.790
familiar with Futures
market trading,

47
00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:12.680
including some of
the products, risks,

48
00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.140
and safeguards as what was
the difference between

49
00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:18.320
spot and forward prices,
contango and backwardation,

50
00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.720
and the other insights
into market mechanics,

51
00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:25.160
including the roles of
commoditized contracts and margin.

52
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.600
We thank you for joining us.